<mxCell id="rulesBox" value="6 KEY DESIGN RULES FOR STOCK TRADING PLATFORM&#10;&#10;1. CAP THEOREM SPLIT (AP vs CP): Market data viewing (stock prices) is AP (Availability + Partition tolerance) — 100ms stale price is fine, must be highly available for 100M users. Order placement and fund management is CP (Consistency + Partition tolerance) — wrong balance or duplicate trade causes huge financial loss, correctness over uptime. Price path: NSE/BSE → Price Ingester → InfluxDB time-series + Redis PubSub &lt;10ms → WebSocket Gateway fan-out → client push ~20ms total. Order path: Order Svc locks funds SELECT FOR UPDATE SERIALIZABLE → Kafka new_orders → Validator checks KYC/funds/risk/dedup → Kafka verified_orders → Exchange Gateway FIX protocol → NSE/BSE matching engine.&#10;&#10;2. FUND LOCKING WITH SELECT FOR UPDATE SERIALIZABLE: User with ₹50K places BUY order estimated ₹49K → BEGIN TRANSACTION → SELECT available_balance FROM funds WHERE user_id FOR UPDATE (pessimistic row lock blocks concurrent transactions) → UPDATE available_balance -= 49K, locked_balance += 49K → COMMIT. Second simultaneous order waits on row lock, reads updated balance ₹820, insufficient funds → ROLLBACK 400 error. No overdraft race condition. On EXECUTED: actual cost ₹49.002K → unlock ₹49K, deduct difference ₹2 from available. On REJECTED/CANCELLED: unlock ₹49K back to available.&#10;&#10;3. WEBSOCKET PERSISTENT CONNECTIONS FOR LIVE PRICES (NOT HTTP POLLING): HTTP polling = 10M users × 1 req/sec = 10M req/sec overhead (99% empty responses asking are we there yet). WebSocket = persistent bidirectional TCP connection, server pushes price updates ONLY when stock changes, ONLY to users watching that stock. Bandwidth efficiency: user subscribes to 10 stocks in watchlist, server pushes only those 10, not all 8-10K stocks. Latency: Exchange tick → Price Ingester → Redis PubSub in-memory fan-out &lt;10ms → WebSocket Gateway → client ~20ms total (well under 50ms target). Sticky session: user_id hash keeps same user on same WebSocket server instance (preserves connection state). Fallback on disconnect: exponential backoff reconnect (1s → 2s → 4s max 30s), GET REST API to fetch missed updates.&#10;&#10;4. ORDER STATE MACHINE WITH KAFKA ASYNC PROCESSING: PENDING (order placed, funds locked) → VERIFIED (passed KYC/funds/risk/circuit breaker/dedup checks) → PLACED (sent to NSE/BSE via FIX protocol) → EXECUTED (matched on exchange, trade confirmed) OR REJECTED (failed validation at any stage). Each state transition published to Kafka topics: new_orders → Validator consumes → verified_orders → Exchange Gateway consumes → order_status → Order Tracker + Portfolio + Notification consume in parallel. Async decoupling: Order Svc returns 200 PENDING in &lt;10ms (user not blocked), downstream processing continues async. At-least-once delivery: Kafka guarantees no order lost, consumer offset not committed until processing complete. Validator 5 instances 50 partitions for horizontal scale.&#10;&#10;5. FIX PROTOCOL WITH 400-500 PERSISTENT CONNECTIONS: Financial Information eXchange (FIX) binary TCP protocol used by all exchanges globally. Tag 35=D NewOrderSingle (place order), Tag 35=8 ExecutionReport (trade confirmation). Exchange Gateway maintains 400-500 persistent FIX connections to NSE/BSE grouped by symbol (RELIANCE, TCS, INFY on same connection) → reuse connections, avoid TCP handshake overhead ~3-5ms per order. Connection pooling: load balance orders across connections by symbol hash. Matching engine on exchange is sequential per stock symbol (one auctioneer per stock prevents duplicate matches) with in-memory order book sorted by price-time priority. Trade execution: buyer highest bid price meets seller lowest ask price → match fires → both orders EXECUTED.&#10;&#10;6. ON-THE-FLY P&amp;L CALCULATION (NEVER STORE CURRENT_VALUE): Portfolio holdings table stores: quantity, avg_buy_price, total_investment (static fields). Current_value and profit_loss calculated fresh on every GET /api/v1/portfolio by reading Redis GET stock_price:RELIANCE (current price refreshed every second by Price Ingester). Formula: current_value = quantity × current_price, profit_loss = current_value - total_investment. WHY never store: price changes hundreds of times per second, stored value stale instantly, users would see incorrect P&amp;L. Redis price cache TTL 1s refreshed continuously. InfluxDB stores historical prices for charts: 1-min candles forever, auto-downsamples to 5-min → hourly → daily candles. Chart query: mean(price) GROUP BY time(5m) last 1 day returns 288 points in &lt;100ms. Circuit breaker: if stock price moves &gt;10% in 5 min → reject all new orders (market protection during extreme volatility, SEBI regulatory requirement)." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e3f2fd;strokeColor=#1976d2;strokeWidth=3;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=12" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="810" width="1780" height="260" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="analogy" value="BEGINNER ANALOGY: Stock Market as Fruit Wholesale Market&#10;&#10;Imagine a massive fruit wholesale market where buyers and sellers meet. The ORDER BOOK is the chalkboard where buyers write Ill buy 10 kg mangoes for ₹50/kg on one side, sellers write Ill sell 10 kg for ₹50/kg on other side — sorted by best prices. When best buyer price meets best seller price, auctioneer (MATCHING ENGINE) yells SOLD and trade happens. Without this central chalkboard, buyers and sellers would never find each other (millions of orders per second across 8000 fruit types impossible). The PRICE BOARD above the market hall shows live mango price updated every second — viewers can watch for free (AP highly available), but actual trading requires KYC-verified account with money locked upfront (CP consistent funds). WEBSOCKET is like loudspeakers that announce MANGO price changed to ₹51 instantly to everyone watching, instead of you walking to the board every second to check (HTTP polling). FIX PROTOCOL is the standardized hand signals auctioneers worldwide use so fruit from any country can trade seamlessly. CIRCUIT BREAKER is the emergency alarm: if mango price drops 10% in 5 minutes (panic selling), auctioneer pauses trading for 15 minutes until everyone calms down — prevents market crash." style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff9c4;strokeColor=#f57f17;strokeWidth=2;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacing=10" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="1110" width="1780" height="140" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="capNote" value="CAP SPLIT: Market Data Viewing = AP (stale 100ms OK, must be available). Order Placement / Fund Management = CP (consistency critical, wrong balance or duplicate trade = financial loss)." style="text;html=1;strokeColor=#ef6c00;fillColor=#fff3e0;align=center;fontSize=11;rounded=1;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="440" width="720" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

<mxCell id="scaleNote" value="SCALE: 1M users, 8-10K stocks (NSE+BSE), 100K orders/min peak, &lt;100ms order placement, &lt;50ms market data latency, 99.99% uptime during market hours 9:15 AM-3:30 PM IST" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=#7b1fa2;fillColor=#f3e5f5;align=center;fontSize=11;rounded=1" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="320" y="500" width="720" height="40" as="geometry"/></mxCell>

</root></mxGraphModel></diagram></mxfile>
